{% block install %}
## Install

Build from source with a recent stable Rust (1.92 or newer):

```sh
cargo build --release
```

This produces two programs in `target/release`:

- `tonewired`, the background daemon that does the audio work
- `tonewire`, the graph editor

On Linux the PipeWire backend is behind a feature flag and needs the PipeWire development headers (`libpipewire-0.3-dev` on Debian and Ubuntu, `pipewire-devel` on Fedora) plus `clang`:

```sh
cargo build --release --features tonewired/pipewire
```

To start the daemon at login, run `tonewired --autostart on`. On Linux you can use the systemd user unit in `packaging/linux/` instead.
{% endblock %}

{% block usage %}
## Usage

Start `tonewire`. It launches the daemon if it isn't running, then shows the graph. Closing the editor leaves the daemon and your routing running; quit from the tray icon to stop it.

Right-click the canvas to add nodes, then drag between pins to connect them. A typical voice chain:

```
Device input -> Noise gate -> Equalizer (low shelf +4 dB) -> De-esser -> Compressor -> Virtual mic
```

Pick "Virtual mic" as the microphone in Discord, OBS or any other app to hear the processed voice.

### Nodes

| Group | Nodes |
|---|---|
| Inputs & outputs | Device input (mic, line in, or what a playback device is playing), Device output, Virtual speaker, Virtual mic, App source |
| Routing | Mixer, Splitter, Gain, Pan |
| Tone | 5-band equalizer |
| Dynamics | De-esser, Compressor, Noise gate, Limiter |
| AI | Noise suppression (RNNoise), NVIDIA denoiser, dereverb, Studio Voice and Speaker Focus |
| Analysis | Meter |

Any output can feed several inputs, and several outputs can feed one input (they are summed). Mono and stereo are converted automatically.

### Per-app routing

An App source node pulls specific programs into the graph. In Move mode the app is heard only through the graph; in Tap mode the graph gets a copy and the app keeps playing as before. A Virtual mic node can also list apps that should record from it.

### Platform notes

- **Linux (PipeWire):** virtual speakers and mics are created by tonewire and show up in every app's device list.
- **Windows (WASAPI):** virtual devices come from a virtual cable driver you install, such as [VB-CABLE](https://vb-audio.com/Cable/) or [Virtual Audio Driver](https://github.com/VirtualDrivers/Virtual-Audio-Driver). Moving apps between devices uses the same interface as Windows' "App volume and device preferences" page; if it is unavailable, App source falls back to Tap.
- **NVIDIA effects** need the [NVIDIA Audio Effects SDK](https://docs.nvidia.com/maxine/afx/latest/index.html), installed separately, and a 48 kHz engine. Set `nvafx_sdk` in `settings.toml` if it lives somewhere unusual. NVIDIA supports desktop use on Windows; on Linux it is experimental.

### Files

The graph is saved to `graph.toml` and engine settings to `settings.toml` in the config directory (`~/.config/tonewire` on Linux, `%APPDATA%\tonewire\config` on Windows). If the graph file can't be read it is moved to `graph.toml.bad` and the daemon starts with an empty graph.
{% endblock %}

{% block extra %}
## Development

```sh
cargo test --workspace                                  # runs on the built-in null backend
cargo run --release -p tonewire-graph --example bench  # DSP cost of a voice chain
cargo run -p tonewired -- --backend null --no-tray     # daemon without an audio system
```

The daemon owns the graph and does all audio processing; the editor is a thin client talking to it over a local socket (a named pipe on Windows). Code on the audio thread never allocates or locks; `crates/graph/tests/engine.rs` checks this with a counting allocator.
{% endblock %}
